Subject: [PATCH 09292/11362] Record the throughput answer and the confound
 that a dead lever exposed

Two findings from the overnight measurements, neither previously written down.

THE THROUGHPUT ANSWER, and it is a config change rather than a migration.
Measured on the scored Blackwell card at production request shape: llama.cpp
batches decode 4.37x to k=16 with MTP OFF (52.2 -> 228.3 tok/s), and 1.15x with
MTP ON. MTP wins single-stream 1.81x and loses at k=16 by 2.1x. With 25 game
threads sharing slots the aggregate is the currency, so the scored path wants
--parallel 16 with MTP off. Against ~100 inductions at a 61k median needing
~148 tok/s to fit the 11.5h cap, 228 clears it. The shipped config gets neither
side of the trade: MTP is off AND --parallel is never passed, so it runs the
k=1 arm at 52.2.

That closes the vLLM question. The case for migrating rested on "llama.cpp does
not batch", and it does. A 3.7 GB wheel closure was resolved and uploaded as
insurance and is not needed -- the correct outcome for insurance.

Four invalid predecessors are recorded in the claim rather than quietly dropped,
because the shape repeats: wrong GPU (the bench kernel omitted the competition
source that provisions Blackwell), then two arms at a 32:1 prompt:output ratio
where prefill dominates and caps the observable effect at ~1.7x regardless of
engine behaviour, then an arm that reshaped the prompt but left max_tokens as a
ceiling and got 190 tokens against a requested 8192. Only the fifth, with
ignore_eos forcing the count and finish_reason captured per request, measured
the regime production lives in.

THE CONFOUND, which is the more valuable finding. The paired goal-defect A/B ran
control on GPU 0 and treatment on GPU 1 for the entire corpus. Treatment was
faster on 10 of 11 pairs, mean ratio 0.922 -- because GPU 1 is 8-11% faster on
this workload, not because of any treatment effect.

It was caught only because the lever turned out to be DEAD: byte-identical
engines across arms proved no treatment effect could exist, so the timing gap
had to come from somewhere else. Had the lever been live, that same 8-11% would
have looked exactly like a plausible mechanism -- the flags letting the model
abandon a dead goal early -- and would likely have been reported as one. An
invalid run exposed a defect a valid run would have hidden. Any re-run must
alternate GPU assignment mid-corpus.

